a='content/os/01-processes/proc-process-abstraction.md';b=a.replace('.md','.questions.json');src=['https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man3/mallopt.3.html','https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man3/malloc_trim.3.html','https://docs.kernel.org/arch/x86/x86_64/mm.html','https://docs.kernel.org/arch/x86/pti.html','https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man3/pthread_create.3.html','https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/proc_pid_stat.5.html']
for old,new in [('A typical Linux x86-64 process looks like this (low addresses at the bottom):','The following is a schematic of a conventional Linux x86-64 layout (low addresses at the bottom), not an exact map. With four-level paging the low user range spans 128 TiB; a large non-canonical gap separates it from high kernel addresses. Five-level paging supports a larger range.'),('Ten `bash` processes share one physical copy of the code. Writing to it segfaults.','Processes using the same executable can share file-backed code pages. An ordinary write to a read-only mapping faults; mappings and permissions can differ.'),('glibc\'s `malloc` serves large requests (by default 128 KiB or more) with separate `mmap` calls instead, so they can be returned to the OS individually.','glibc may use separate `mmap` allocations for requests not served by its free lists above a threshold initially 128 KiB and normally adjusted dynamically. Arenas and tunables also affect placement.'),('Its default limit on Linux is usually 8 MiB (`ulimit -s` shows 8192 KiB).','Its limit is environment-dependent; an illustrative 8 MiB limit appears as 8192 KiB in `ulimit -s`.'),('**Page zero is unmapped**, so dereferencing `NULL` faults rather than silently reading data.','**Page zero is normally unmapped** on ordinary Linux deployments. Null dereference is undefined in C, so the language itself does not guarantee a particular signal.'),('**The kernel half** is mapped in every process but marked supervisor-only, so user code faults if it touches it. On x86-64 with 4-level paging, user space spans 128 TiB.','**Kernel mappings** are inaccessible to ordinary user-mode code. With KPTI, the user page tables contain only minimal kernel-entry mappings; the full kernel map is selected on entry.'),('const char *s = "hi"; /* rodata */','const char *s = "hi"; /* pointer: data */'),('On Linux the printed addresses go up in the order shown: text lowest, then data and BSS, then the heap, and the stack far higher (around `0x7ffc...`). With ASLR, the exact numbers change on every run.','This illustrates one conventional executable layout; exact ordering and locations depend on the ABI, linker, allocator and ASLR settings. ASLR does not promise a different value on every run.'),("`s[0] = 'H'` crashes; `s = \"yo\"` is fine.","Writing through this `const char *` is rejected without a cast; modifying the literal through a writable-typed pointer is undefined behaviour and commonly faults. Reassigning `s = \"yo\"` is valid."),('At most one process per core.','At most one executing thread per logical CPU at an instant; an SMT core can execute several hardware threads.'),('A process stuck in `D` can\'t even be killed with `SIGKILL` until the I/O completes.','A task in a truly uninterruptible wait cannot act on `SIGKILL` until the wait ends. The displayed `D` state can also cover killable waits that do respond to fatal signals; it is not a complete description of the wait primitive.')]:edit(a,old,new,'Correct memory-layout, allocator and scheduler assumptions.',src)
qe(b,'proc-process-abstraction-q2','prompt','Assume an unoptimised conventional Linux C build where these objects are retained and automatic variables use stack storage. In which region does each live?\n\n```c\nint a = 5;\nstatic int b;\nvoid f(void) {\n  int c = 1;\n  int *d = malloc(4);\n}\n```\n\nAssume allocation succeeds; `*d` labels the allocated four bytes.','Compilers can eliminate unused objects or keep locals in registers.',src)
qe(b,'proc-process-abstraction-q4','prompt','A program retains and uses `static char buffer[200 * 1024 * 1024];` (200 MiB, no initialiser). A conventional ELF build stores it in BSS, and the binary is only 20 KB. Why?','Exclude optimisation eliminating the unused array.',src)
d=json.loads(Path(b).read_text());q=next(q for q in d if q['id'].endswith('-q4'))
qe(b,q['id'],'workedExample',q['workedExample'].replace('At `exec`, the kernel reserves the virtual range and backs it with the shared zero page.','The loader establishes a zero-initialised virtual range; anonymous pages can be populated lazily, with read faults using a shared zero page where supported.').replace('Real physical pages are allocated only when the program writes to them (a page fault per first touch).','Private physical pages are normally needed on writes; huge pages and other kernel policies affect fault granularity.'),'Distinguish virtual mapping from eager zero-page installation.',src)
qe(b,'proc-process-abstraction-q5','prompt','Assume the main thread has an 8 MiB Linux stack limit and a recursive function actually consumes about 1 KiB per call, without tail-call elimination. Roughly how deep can it recurse before exhausting the stack?','Make stack limit and frame-size assumptions explicit.',src)
qe(b,'proc-process-abstraction-q5','workedExample','8 MiB / 1 KiB = 8,192 nominal frames, with fewer available after existing stack use. Access beyond the expandable stack can generate SIGSEGV. Check the actual resource limit. Under Linux NPTL, default new-thread stack size derives from the startup RLIMIT_STACK setting when finite, or an architecture-specific fallback when unlimited; pthread_attr_setstacksize explicitly overrides an attribute.','Correct pthread stack-default description.',src)
qe(b,'proc-process-abstraction-q6','prompt','What is the C-language status of this code (assume `<stdio.h>` is included)?\n\n```c\nint main(void) {\n  char *s = "hello";\n  s[0] = \'H\';\n  puts(s);\n}\n```','Undefined behaviour does not guarantee SIGSEGV.',src)
qe(b,'proc-process-abstraction-q6','text','Undefined behaviour from modifying a string literal; on conventional Linux builds the write commonly faults with SIGSEGV','Give language guarantee separately from common observation.',src,option=3)
qe(b,'proc-process-abstraction-q6','explanation','C forbids modifying a string literal. A read-only mapping commonly traps the write, but undefined behaviour has no guaranteed output or signal.','Correct undefined-behaviour guarantee.',src,option=3)
qe(b,'proc-process-abstraction-q6','workedExample','The literal is not modifiable in C. Conventional ELF builds often put it in read-only storage, so an executed write commonly faults; optimisations and undefined behaviour prevent a guaranteed outcome. Use `char s[] = "hello";` for a modifiable automatic array.','Keep worked result consistent with UB.',src)
qe(b,'proc-process-abstraction-q10','text','A separately mmap-backed allocation can be unmapped independently, while arena allocations may remain cached or fragmented after free; placement and reclamation depend on allocator settings','glibc small allocations can use mmap-backed arenas and madvise reclamation.',src,option=3)
qe(b,'proc-process-abstraction-q10','explanation','The initial mmap threshold is 128 KiB but normally adapts, and free lists can satisfy larger requests. Retained arena capacity can explain RSS behaviour; it is not a rule that every small free stays resident.','Qualify dynamic threshold/free-list condition.',src,option=3)
qe(b,'proc-process-abstraction-q10','workedExample','A large allocation serviced by its own mapping can be released with munmap. Small allocations may leave free chunks among live arena allocations, so the allocator retains capacity for reuse. The brk boundary only shrinks from the top, but glibc can also use mmap-backed arenas and malloc_trim/madvise to release whole free pages inside arenas. Inspect the actual allocation path; size alone does not guarantee placement or RSS behaviour.','Correct false claim that glibc cannot return interior free pages.',src)
save([a,b])
